WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.820
Did humans breed dogs to be friendly?

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.540
Compared with wolves, dogs are hyper-social--

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:10.480
they go out of their way to seek attention
and affection from others, particularly humans.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.060
But dogs aren’t the only ones who are predisposed
towards being friendly.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.840
There does seem to be a genetic component
to sociability in people --

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.480
Humans with Williams-Beuren Syndrome are also
known to be hypersocial,

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.740
with affected children often running up and
hugging people at random.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.120
Scientists were curious if this same genes
involved in WBS

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.000
are also involved in sociability in dogs.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.920
To find out, researchers tested 18 dogs and
10 wolves

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.360
for traits related to sociability with humans.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.160
In one experiment, a person sat in the middle
of an enclosure,

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.740
while either dogs or wolves were nearby.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:50.000
During the 8 minutes of testing, the dogs
spent 53 percent of their time

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.920
within close proximity of the unfamiliar human.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:59.070
The wolves were loners, only walking by the
unknown person 35 percent of the time.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:03.780
It turns out that canine chromosome 6 appears
to have a section analogous to the genes in

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:08.300
humans that, when absent, cause Williams-Beuren
Syndrome.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:13.140
Peering into the gene data, scientists saw
that hypersocial dogs had more DNA disruptions

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.900
in this region than the more aloof wolves,

00:01:16.909 --> 00:01:20.990
The researchers say that while individual
experience is equally important to genetics

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:22.909
when it comes to sociability,

00:01:22.909 --> 00:01:24.960
the findings suggest that the genes in question

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.300
may have contributed to the rapid domestication of dogs.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:31.540
And show that ancient genetic engineering
may be one reason

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.080
why your pooch loves to see you come home every day.

